{
  "image_name": "4e57bb73058d066a45f7460b4ef52e81.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates a proposed sequence by which a B cell crosses the brain endothelium into the perivascular space, beneath a layer of astrocytes. The cell moves through the blood vessel lumen from left to right and undergoes the classic steps of immune-cell trafficking: first rolling along the endothelial surface, then receiving chemokine signals, activating integrins, becoming tethered more firmly, and finally squeezing between endothelial cells by diapedesis.\n\nThe diagram emphasizes the molecular cues thought to guide each stage. Rolling is linked to ALCAM and possibly PSGL1. Chemokine signaling appears central to the transition from loose contact to firm adhesion, with several candidate chemokines listed, including CXCL13, CXCL12, CXCL10, CCL19, and CCL21, plus additional uncertain signals. After this stimulation, integrin activation enables stronger adhesion, and the B cell flattens and deforms as it attaches to the endothelial wall. The adhesion and transmigration step is associated with ALCAM, VLA4, and possibly LFA1.\n\nOverall, the image presents a stepwise model of B-cell recruitment across the blood-brain barrier or a similar CNS vascular interface, highlighting both the cellular route and the key adhesion and chemokine interactions involved.",
